    <p>In a large program, writing statements outside a function can
      lead to poor organization. Professional software developers write
      statements inside a function whenever possible. Beginning with this
      lesson, you will do the following in each program:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <div>Write nearly all statements inside a user-defined
          function.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>Write a user-defined function named <code>main</code>,
          which contains the beginning statements of your
          program.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>Write one or more user-defined functions that have
          parameters, perform calculations and other useful work, and
          return a result to the call point.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>Write a call to the <code>main</code> function at the
          bottom of your program.</div>
      </li>
    </ol>
